WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:04.860
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.160
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.440
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:08.700
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.520
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:19.500
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:20.660
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:26.750
It's time.

9
00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:27.730
It is time.

10
00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:31.410
We have arrived at Threat Level Midnight.

11
00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:34.590
It is Season 7, Episode 17.

12
00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:38.290
It was directed by Tucker Gates, written by B.J.

13
00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:40.890
Novak, and guess who is here with us today?

14
00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:41.810
B.J.

15
00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:42.710
Novak!

16
00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:43.210
Woo-hoo!

17
00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:46.990
All the Tucker Gates fans are like, oh, okay, that's okay, too.

18
00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:48.190
But, okay.

19
00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:53.490
B.J., we're so excited that you're going to talk about this episode with us.

20
00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:56.130
I absolutely loved rewatching it.

21
00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.970
I watched the extended version on the DVD.

22
00:00:59.310 --> 00:00:59.630
Great.

23
00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:00.770
Which is also amazing.

24
00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:02.550
I watched the extended version.

25
00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:06.530
And then I went back and I watched The Client because I was like, I need to know where it all began.

26
00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:07.710
I need to see it again.

27
00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:09.590
Oh, my God.

28
00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:13.210
I forgot so many different twists and turns of Michael's part.

29
00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:24.830
The great thing that we discovered with The Client, I feel, is how many things you learn about someone by watching something they didn't know was revealing something about them.

30
00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:31.810
You know, so Michael's screenplay, like, I forget it, brother, and that, oh, wow, okay, he changed Dwight.

31
00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:33.530
You know, all these little things.

32
00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:35.570
It's not just what a stupid screenplay.

33
00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:45.010
It's you actually get a window into who he was writing it, which is so cute and hard to watch, which is like a lot of Michael Scott.

34
00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:50.730
But, so, yeah, I, I, let me just say how happy I am that you brought me on to talk about this.

35
00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:55.730
This is my happiest experience probably with an episode.

36
00:01:55.910 --> 00:02:01.330
And it's the one that I, especially the last time you had me on about Scott Stotts and it was like, all right, let's get into it.

37
00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:03.930
That's the least comfortable one that I worked on.

38
00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:11.670
This is the most joyous, like, this was really a victory lap for Steve and the whole cast, for me and the writers.

39
00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:16.350
It was just like, ugh, like, it was really a pure joyous episode.

40
00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:18.270
We could have done a two-hour version.

41
00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:21.690
So I'm, I'm just happy to, to reminisce about it.

42
00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:27.650
Angela, weren't you saying that on the commentary Dave Rogers was talking about the joy?

43
00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:33.730
Yes, on the DVD commentary, there are you, Craig Robinson, Creed Bratton, Charlie Grandy,

44
00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:39.950
and Dave Rogers, and Dave shared how jazzed everyone was after the table read.

45
00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:46.010
He said the table read was so electric and everyone was so into it and having such a ball.

46
00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:50.970
Steve was so into it and everyone left feeling super pumped after the table read.

47
00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:53.030
They couldn't wait to dive in this episode.

48
00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:55.090
Like, all departments were so excited.

49
00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:57.130
Yeah, and you see that in the episode.

50
00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:03.130
Well, BJ, where did the idea for doing Threat Level Midnight as a full episode come from?

51
00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:04.470
It came directly from Steve.

52
00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:09.350
When we went into the final season, I, I might be conflating memories, but as I remember it,

53
00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:15.830
Greg asked Steve, Greg Daniels asked him, is there anything you want to do before you leave?

54
00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:18.110
And he said, let me think about it.

55
00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:24.830
And then one day I remember I was walking from the writer's building to the part of the parking lot

56
00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:26.530
where you run into each other, right?

57
00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:27.630
Yeah, yeah, right.

58
00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:31.270
Like, between, like, the writer's office, which is part of Dunderbilt and the other part.

59
00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:35.710
And Steve is there, like, with his suit and untucked shirt, you know.

60
00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:38.890
Steve was like, hey, I realized the episode I want to do.

61
00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:41.530
I want to see Threat Level Midnight.

62
00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:45.470
And I was so excited to run back and tell the writers.

63
00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:48.550
And it's like, you know when you get news before anyone else?

64
00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:51.050
Like, I had just received this news.

65
00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:51.970
You got the little nugget.

66
00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:53.150
I was like, I talked to Steve.

67
00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:56.210
Like, Steve, here's what Steve wants to do.

68
00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:59.230
Threat Level Midnight and everyone lit into a big grin.

69
00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:01.130
It's like, we can grant that wish.

70
00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:02.470
That is a great wish.

71
00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:06.050
And probably because I reported it with such excitement,

72
00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:08.750
it seemed like I was the guy to write it.

73
00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:10.130
And I was.

74
00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:11.150
You know, I got the assignment.

75
00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:12.230
I was going to ask you that.

76
00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.270
How did you get assigned this script?

77
00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:18.130
Do you think it was because you're the person who ran into him in the parking lot?

78
00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:20.410
I think the fact that I came in with that big grin,

79
00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:22.970
and that grin was intoxicating to the room.

80
00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:25.010
I was the right messenger of it.

81
00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:28.930
But I also think because I had been there from the client.

82
00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:30.430
Not all the writers were still there.

83
00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:33.130
It was only a few of us that had been there back then.

84
00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:35.270
And it was really like a full circle.

85
00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:39.130
It was also my swan song, the way it was Steve's, as a writer.

86
00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:40.990
That's the kind of thing I loved writing.

87
00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:44.570
I had been kind of in a rut writing, in my opinion.

88
00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:46.190
I mean, the viewers don't notice this stuff

89
00:04:46.190 --> 00:04:48.890
because they all end up being written collectively anyway.

90
00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:51.790
But I'm like assigned Prince family paper.

91
00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:53.250
I don't remember what it is exactly.

92
00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:56.530
Just like, I'm like, what happened to the, that's what she said,

93
00:04:56.710 --> 00:04:58.450
like the greatest hits, you know?

94
00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:01.570
And just like a big, funny, juicy topic.

95
00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:04.830
And so to have Threat Level Midnight, it kind of was like,

96
00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:06.650
there's got to be some metaphor there,

97
00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:10.710
but like, you know, time travel to me.

98
00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:14.390
Like back to the early days of like anything can happen.

99
00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:18.270
And, you know, these characters are so wild and yet so rich.

100
00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:20.210
And it was, and it was comedy.

101
00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:22.870
It wasn't, we didn't have too much drama to service.

102
00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:27.870
Although the poignancy of Michael having made this ridiculous movie,

103
00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:33.170
that he has invested all of this fantasy in the idea that it's great.

104
00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:35.130
And we, we all have that thing, you know,

105
00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:37.970
the novel that you've, you're going to write someday,

106
00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:39.270
the screenplay you're going to write someday,

107
00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:40.590
the role you're going to play someday, you know,

108
00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:45.670
and not just us in the business, everybody pretty much has that thing.

109
00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:47.530
They, they think they might write someday.

110
00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:53.930
And they'd be very crushed if it were read and declared not good.

111
00:05:54.430 --> 00:05:55.970
You know, we cling to that.

112
00:05:56.150 --> 00:05:57.690
And so Michael had that.

113
00:05:57.790 --> 00:06:03.450
And so the poignancy of something that to us is the silliest thing we've ever seen

114
00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:04.250
in the best way.

115
00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:07.110
And to him is like, that's his masterpiece.

116
00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:13.710
If he doesn't have that, he's not, you know, not a secret genius, not talented.

117
00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:18.110
That made it poignant, but there wasn't, it wasn't like, you know,

118
00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:23.310
Jim and Karen getting serious, you know, it wasn't any one of those plots.

119
00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:29.830
I loved watching it just because it made me miss the early seasons.

120
00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:32.110
We're having so much fun re-watching the show.

121
00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:32.690
That's what I mean.

122
00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:35.110
It was, it was an early season vibe.

123
00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:39.390
Yeah, and just even from like the conference room scene in the very beginning

124
00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:42.850
where everyone's like laughing at the movie and he gets his feelings hurt.

125
00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:43.190
Yeah.

126
00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:44.530
And you're dressed as Pam.

127
00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:48.730
I mean, you're dressed as like OG Pam with your cardigan and your hair

128
00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:49.510
and the whole thing.

129
00:06:49.610 --> 00:06:49.790
Yeah.

130
00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:54.030
And you just felt that sort of Jim and Pam, I don't know, early days.

131
00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:58.730
It made me nostalgic to want to go back and start the re-watch over again.

132
00:06:58.730 --> 00:07:05.150
Yeah, that was a really smart thing, this late in the run of the show to give us like this,

133
00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:13.170
this travel back in time to Pam sitting next to Roy on the floor.

134
00:07:13.390 --> 00:07:17.630
And you're like, oh my gosh, look how far their lives have come.

135
00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:18.110
I know.

136
00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:22.330
And then, and then to cut back to Jim and Pam when like Jim is golden face and he,

137
00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:24.810
Pam looks at him like, oh my God, I can't believe you did that.

138
00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:27.950
Like you, you see so much, so much growth.

139
00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:28.810
Right, things he wouldn't have done then.

140
00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:34.690
And like the people doing the scarn is so interesting because it's like who he did and

141
00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:39.910
didn't get, like I think Rashida is in it, right?

142
00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:40.830
Yeah.

143
00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:43.890
That's the last person that would have done this.

144
00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:45.110
And yet, you know what?

145
00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:50.290
At that moment, he was her boss and it wasn't that stupid a thing to do, I guess.

146
00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:51.450
Didn't take up too much time.

147
00:07:51.590 --> 00:07:52.530
So here we go.

148
00:07:52.630 --> 00:07:53.250
We learn the dance.

149
00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:57.150
You know, it's funny, it's cute that some people would have done it.

150
00:07:57.470 --> 00:07:59.630
Angela, your character does the scarn.

151
00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:01.990
Oh yes, I do the scarn.

152
00:08:02.390 --> 00:08:06.810
Yes, you doing the scarn is even wilder, but like it's your boss.

153
00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:12.330
What's really funny is I went back and read the shooting draft and it said that when you

154
00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:16.290
cut to the bachelorette group in the corner, it's Meredith, Phyllis, Karen, and Angela.

155
00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:16.530
Right.

156
00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:19.470
Angela has brought her work and is doing work.

157
00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:21.810
She has papers, like accounting papers.

158
00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:23.350
So she's sort of begrudgingly there.

159
00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:23.910
Okay, okay.

160
00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:29.170
And then we each have a line when the bartender, you know, when Andy's like, the bachelorette

161
00:08:29.170 --> 00:08:30.450
party sent you over a cocktail.

162
00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:34.330
I actually printed out what we said over in the bachelorette corner.

163
00:08:34.330 --> 00:08:39.490
So both Meredith and Phyllis go, hey, and then Meredith goes, who ordered the man burger?

164
00:08:39.730 --> 00:08:40.330
Well done.

165
00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:44.230
And Phyllis says, hey, hot stuff, one last fling.

166
00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:48.090
And then Angela says, come and ride the choo-choo.

167
00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:53.390
And then in parentheses, it says, pained the sex choo-choo.

168
00:08:54.410 --> 00:08:55.510
That sounds right.

169
00:08:55.510 --> 00:08:58.750
And then it cuts to Karen's line and that's what made it in.

170
00:08:58.850 --> 00:08:59.710
But we filmed that.

171
00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:02.010
I remember filming the sex choo-choo.

172
00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:04.470
It's in the extended DVD version.

173
00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:04.870
That's right.

174
00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:07.790
That whole bachelorette scene is in that version.

175
00:09:08.150 --> 00:09:11.950
And Andy is like such a ham, of course.

176
00:09:12.050 --> 00:09:12.950
Oh my God.

177
00:09:13.090 --> 00:09:14.790
As the bartender, which he would be.

178
00:09:15.350 --> 00:09:15.650
Yeah.

179
00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:18.990
So yeah, that was very fun doing all that math.

180
00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:28.610
But I think a lot of it, which is sweet and part of the spirit of the office too, is that people just do things when someone is excited about it.

181
00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:31.530
And I think it's why Michael Scott was a good salesman.

182
00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:33.070
And it's why the people at the bar did discard.

183
00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:38.550
A guy comes in with wild eyes and little boy energy and tells you everything he's going to do.

184
00:09:38.650 --> 00:09:39.550
And here's what you're going to do.

185
00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:40.630
And here's how great it's going to be.

186
00:09:40.870 --> 00:09:42.750
And you're just like, okay, sure.

187
00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:46.950
It's harder to cross your arms and be like, no, thank you.

188
00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:49.690
Some people do that, but people just do things.

189
00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:51.330
And that's what you sent me last night.

190
00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:57.130
The David Wallace talking head that got cut where he says, I should have done it.

191
00:09:57.510 --> 00:10:05.310
You know, like, yeah, I think at the end of the day, you look back on things and you, you, the people who do say, I'm not doing that.

192
00:10:05.410 --> 00:10:05.910
They regret it.

193
00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:06.950
Like life is short.

194
00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:08.170
No one's paying attention to what you do.

195
00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:15.250
Like, even if you, you, David Wallace shouldn't be in this movie, like whatever, you'll all not work here someday.

196
00:10:15.430 --> 00:10:16.050
Just say yes.

197
00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:18.130
You know, that energy,

198
00:10:18.530 --> 00:10:29.650
that little boy wild eyed energy where someone gets an idea and then you end up doing something that you didn't think you were going to do is literally every situation I've ever been in with John Krasinski.

199
00:10:30.110 --> 00:10:30.270
Yeah.

200
00:10:30.430 --> 00:10:30.550
Right.

201
00:10:30.950 --> 00:10:33.950
Doesn't John, he'll can be like, Jenna, get Lee.

202
00:10:34.310 --> 00:10:35.790
We're going to Vegas this weekend.

203
00:10:36.030 --> 00:10:38.490
I'm like, we are, we're going to Vegas.

204
00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:39.510
He's like, we're doing it.

205
00:10:39.610 --> 00:10:40.490
I have tickets.

206
00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:42.610
I'm like, you have a ticket in my name.

207
00:10:42.930 --> 00:10:43.350
No, no, no.

208
00:10:43.510 --> 00:10:44.370
I have tickets.

209
00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:45.430
You get tickets.

210
00:10:45.650 --> 00:10:46.310
We're going.

211
00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:47.910
Wait, I didn't even hear about this trip.

212
00:10:48.130 --> 00:10:49.590
Did this trip happen or is this?

213
00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:50.930
This was a real trip.

214
00:10:51.010 --> 00:10:51.410
Yes.

215
00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:51.950
Yes.

216
00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:56.710
One, we're standing out in the parking lot of Dunder Mifflin.

217
00:10:56.870 --> 00:10:58.430
We're doing some scene.

218
00:10:59.490 --> 00:11:00.430
John's on the phone.

219
00:11:00.610 --> 00:11:01.550
He comes back.

220
00:11:01.690 --> 00:11:03.070
He's like, what are you doing this weekend?

221
00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:04.210
I'm like, I don't know.

222
00:11:04.310 --> 00:11:06.350
I think Lee and I might, he goes, you're going to Vegas.

223
00:11:06.610 --> 00:11:07.990
We're all going to Vegas.

224
00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:09.450
I'm like, we are?

225
00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:10.990
We're going to Vegas?

226
00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:12.530
He's like, yeah, yeah.

227
00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:14.170
Can you make a 630 flight?

228
00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:18.850
And I'm like, I don't, I haven't, and then I call Lee and I'm like, I think we're going

229
00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:19.430
to Vegas?

230
00:11:19.750 --> 00:11:20.550
It's the beSt.

231
00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:21.790
It's why he's so successful.

232
00:11:22.610 --> 00:11:25.590
It's why Michael Scott was successful in his way.

233
00:11:26.270 --> 00:11:30.490
It's all of us should be more like that because think of how it feels on the other side.

234
00:11:30.610 --> 00:11:31.570
I mean, you were that person.

235
00:11:31.570 --> 00:11:35.470
If someone comes up to you with that wild excitement and say, you're going to Vegas, you're like,

236
00:11:35.590 --> 00:11:36.070
we are?

237
00:11:36.770 --> 00:11:37.130
Okay.

238
00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:39.590
I have three things I need to take care of.

239
00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:41.170
How quickly can I, you know?

